### JoisEyes backdoor server — manual request collection
### docs/joiseyes-backdoor-v1.md is the authoritative contract for these endpoints.
###
### Setup (one-time, per machine):
###   1. In the IDE's HTTP Client environment picker (top-right of this file), select "local".
###   2. Create docs/requests/http-client.private.env.json (NOT committed — see .gitignore) with:
###        { "local": { "host": "<your phone's Tailscale IP, e.g. 100.90.80.70>" } }
###      This overrides the placeholder host in http-client.env.json. Keeping your real tailnet
###      IP out of the public repo is a minor precaution, not a secret — there's no auth token.
###
### The server binds only to the phone's Tailscale address (docs/joiseyes-backdoor-v1.md §2) —
### these requests will only succeed from another device on the same tailnet. There is no
### authentication (single-user device); rate limiting (60/min) still applies to every route.

### Liveness check — no sensor reads, cheapest possible.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/health

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", () => client.assert(response.status === 200));
    client.test("has api_version", () => client.assert(response.body.api_version === 1));
%}

### Full snapshot, default params — fast=1 implied: last-known everywhere, no fresh sensor work.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", () => client.assert(response.status === 200));
    client.test("has all top-level sections", () => {
        const b = response.body;
        ["meta", "power", "screen", "dnd", "location", "activity", "environment",
         "wearables", "health_connect", "connectivity", "bt_devices"].forEach(key =>
            client.assert(key in b, `missing key: ${key}`)
        );
    });
%}

### Snapshot with a fresh one-shot GPS fix (~3s). Sets location.fresh=true on success,
### falls back to the last cached fix with fresh=false on timeout.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot?gps=fix

### Snapshot with bt=scan — accepted but currently a no-op this cut; bt_devices stays [].
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot?bt=scan

### Snapshot with gps=fix AND bt=scan combined (worst-case latency, on-demand only).
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot?gps=fix&bt=scan

### hc=pull — triggers a Health Connect ingest + PC sync push chain (docs/joiseyes-backdoor-v1.md
### §3.1). Returns an immediate ack, NOT the full snapshot; watch health_connect.last_sync_ts on
### a follow-up plain /v1/snapshot request to confirm it actually ran.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot?hc=pull

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", () => client.assert(response.status === 200));
    client.test("triggered is present", () => client.assert("triggered" in response.body));
%}

### Re-run hc=pull immediately after the request above — if the chain from the previous
### request is still running, this should ack already_running=true instead of enqueueing a
### second chain (coalescing).
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot?hc=pull

### Follow-up plain snapshot to see whether health_connect.last_sync_ts advanced after hc=pull.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/snapshot

### Unknown route — expect 404.
GET http://{{host}}:{{port}}/v1/nonexistent

> {%
    client.test("status is 404", () => client.assert(response.status === 404));
%}

### Rate limit (60 req/min, docs/joiseyes-backdoor-v1.md §2): not practical to script as a single
### request here. To exercise it manually, use the IDE's "Run all requests in file" repeatedly, or
### from a shell on a tailnet-joined machine:
###   for i in $(seq 1 65); do curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" \
###     http://<host>:8788/v1/snapshot; done
### Expect the first ~60 to return 200 and the rest 429 with a Retry-After header.
